---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CalcMoneyArea from '@/components/tools/everyday/CalcMoneyArea.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="square-footage-calculator">
  <CalcMoneyArea client:load variant="area" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Square footage for several rooms</h2>
    <p>
      A real job is often a lounge, a hallway and a landing, or four rooms of flooring in one order. Add a
      row for each space, mix shapes, and the square footage calculator adds up the total. Export the whole
      list as CSV, Excel or PDF to take to a merchant.
    </p>

    <h2>Area formulas</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[460px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Shape</th>
            <th>Area</th>
            <th>Measure</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Rectangle</td><td><code>length × width</code></td><td>the two longest perpendicular walls</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Square</td><td><code>side²</code></td><td>one side</td></tr>
          <tr><td>L-shape</td><td><code>(long × width) − (cut-out length × cut-out width)</code></td><td>the full rectangle, then the missing corner</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Triangle</td><td><code>(base × height) ÷ 2</code></td><td>height is perpendicular to the base, not a sloping side</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Circle</td><td><code>π × (diameter ÷ 2)²</code></td><td>widest point across</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Trapezoid</td><td><code>((a + b) ÷ 2) × height</code></td><td>the two parallel sides and the distance between them</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Split an awkward shape into two rectangles and add a second row. This is faster and easier to check,
      with less risk of error.
    </p>

    <h2>Feet, inches, metres and feet-and-inches</h2>
    <p>
      The unit you pick applies to every field. Each field also reads feet-and-inches notation, whatever the
      selector says: <code>12' 6"</code>, <code>12ft 6in</code> and <code>12'6</code> all mean 12.5 feet, as
      on a tape measure.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each result appears in square feet, square metres and square yards. The conversion uses the
      international foot of exactly 0.3048 m, so one square foot is exactly 0.09290304 m², with no rounding
      error between the two systems.
    </p>

    <h2>Area conversions</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[360px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>From</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">To</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Multiply by</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>Square feet</td><td style="text-align:right">Square metres</td><td style="text-align:right">0.092903</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Square metres</td><td style="text-align:right">Square feet</td><td style="text-align:right">10.7639</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Square feet</td><td style="text-align:right">Square yards</td><td style="text-align:right">÷ 9</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Square feet</td><td style="text-align:right">Acres</td><td style="text-align:right">÷ 43,560</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Square metres</td><td style="text-align:right">Hectares</td><td style="text-align:right">÷ 10,000</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>How to measure a room</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Measure at the widest and longest points, wall face to wall face, not along the skirting.
      </li>
      <li>
        Measure each dimension twice, at opposite ends of the room. Older buildings are seldom square, and
        a 3-inch difference over 14 feet is normal.
      </li>
      <li>
        Include alcoves and bay windows as their own rows if you are buying material to cover them.
      </li>
      <li>
        Decide whether doorways and thresholds are in or out, and do the same in every room.
      </li>
      <li>
        Write the measurements down before you type them. A second measurement costs little; a wrong order
        costs much more.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Waste allowance</h2>
    <p>
      The area you measure is less than the quantity you buy. Off-cuts, breakages, pattern matching and
      cutting mistakes all use material. Five to ten per cent covers most jobs; use fifteen for diagonal
      layouts, long narrow rooms, or rooms with many doorways and angles. The calculator adds the allowance
      to the net area before it calculates any cost.
    </p>

    <h2>Cost per square foot</h2>
    <p>
      The cost field multiplies the area with waste by the rate you enter: flooring, turf, insulation, rent,
      cleaning or a build estimate. The arithmetic is the same for all of them. For material quantities, the
      paint, tile, flooring and concrete calculators on this site apply their own rules.
    </p>

    <h2>Property square footage is a different question</h2>
    <p>
      For a listing or a valuation, "gross internal area" definitions vary by country and by professional
      standard. Some include stairwells, some exclude garages and unheated space, and some measure to the
      internal wall face and others to the external one. This page calculates geometry; the standard you
      work to decides which surfaces to include.
    </p>

    <h2>Measure a whole house offline</h2>
    <p>
      The page needs no server, so it works in a basement with no signal. Your measurement list is stored only
      if you turn saving on, and then only in this browser, with a delete button next to the switch. Your
      device makes the CSV, Excel and PDF exports.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
